“Common sense” only works because it is really shared compressed context.

When that shared context disappears, you cannot safely rely on implication anymore. You need a different stack:

common sense → shared sense → explicit sense-making

A shared sense is the overlap between two or more agents’ models of reality: assumptions, norms, meanings, expectations, and causal beliefs that do not have to be continuously restated.

When even that overlap is uncertain, you fall back to:

* first principles,
* observable evidence,
* explicit definitions,
* incentives and constraints,
* prediction and feedback,
* negotiated rules.

So the answer to “what do you use when common sense is no longer common?” is:

You manufacture common ground.

You make implicit variables explicit until enough agreement exists to coordinate again.

There’s also an important distinction here:

Common sense = “everyone should know this.”
Shared sense = “we know that we mean the same thing by this.”

The second is actually stronger for coordination because it does not pretend universality.

Two people can have very different worldviews and still establish sufficient shared sense to cooperate.

And this loops into the Silly Goose problem: assuming your local common sense is globally common is itself a local-to-global generalization error.

A better rule would be:

Never assume common sense across an unverified boundary. Establish shared sense first.